## 1. `cureLocalIntersections` ✔ verified byte-for-byte

```cpp
// go through all polygon nodes and cure small local self-intersections
template <typename N>
typename Earcut<N>::Node*
Earcut<N>::cureLocalIntersections(Node* start) {
    Node* p = start;
    do {
        Node* a = p->prev;
        Node* b = p->next->next;

        // a, p, p->next, b
        if (!equals(a, b) && intersects(a, p, p->next, b) &&
            locallyInside(a, b) && locallyInside(b, a)) {

            indices.emplace_back(a->i);
            indices.emplace_back(p->i);
            indices.emplace_back(b->i);

            // remove two nodes involved
            removeNode(p);
            removeNode(p->next);

            p = start = b;
        }
        p = p->next;
    } while (p != start);

    return filterPoints(p);
}
```

**Exact emission order: `a->i`, then `p->i`, then `b->i`** — i.e. `prev`, `current`, `next->next`. Verified at `0x402a38/0x402a60/0x402a7f` (three sequential `push_back`s reading `0x24(%r13)=a`, `0x24(%rbp)=p`, `0x24(%r12)=b`).

Critical control-flow details that a naive port gets wrong:

* The guard order is exactly `!equals(a,b)` → `intersects(a,p,p->next,b)` → `locallyInside(a,b)` → `locallyInside(b,a)`, **short-circuited**. `intersects` is only called if `!equals(a,b)`.
* `removeNode(p)` happens **before** `removeNode(p->next)`, and `p->next` is re-read *after* `p` was unlinked (the compiler reloads `0x18(%rbp)` at `0x402ace`). Because `removeNode(p)` does not modify `p`'s own `prev`/`next` fields, `p->next` is still the original successor — but the *second* `removeNode` then operates on a node whose `prev` has already been rewired to `a`. Net effect: both `p` and `p->next` leave the ring, `a->next == b`, `b->prev == a`.
* After the cure, `p = start = b;` and then the loop-tail `p = p->next` runs, so the next node examined is `b->next` and the loop terminates when it wraps back to `b`.
* The return is `filterPoints(p)` with the **default `end = nullptr`** (asm: `xor %esi,%esi`), and at loop exit `p == start`, so `filterPoints(start, nullptr)` is equivalent. `filterPoints` is a tail call.

