# 2. `isEar` — exact body

```cpp
// check whether a polygon node forms a valid ear with adjacent nodes
template <typename N>
bool Earcut<N>::isEar(Node* ear) {
    const Node* a = ear->prev;
    const Node* b = ear;
    const Node* c = ear->next;

    if (area(a, b, c) >= 0) return false; // reflex, can't be an ear

    // now make sure we don't have other points inside the potential ear
    Node* p = ear->next->next;

    while (p != ear->prev) {
        if (pointInTriangle(a->x, a->y, b->x, b->y, c->x, c->y, p->x, p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->next;
    }

    return true;
}
```

- `area(a,b,c) >= 0` → reject (`>=`, not `>`; collinear ears are rejected here).
- Walk starts at `ear->next->next` and stops **before** `ear->prev` (exclusive on both neighbors).
- Reflex test on the candidate interior point is `area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0` (`>=`, so collinear points also block the ear).

**VARIANT B (earcut.js ≥ 2.2.4 / v3):** adds a triangle-bbox short-circuit before `pointInTriangle`:
```cpp
    const double x0 = std::min(ax, std::min(bx, cx)), y0 = std::min(ay, std::min(by, cy));
    const double x1 = std::max(ax, std::max(bx, cx)), y1 = std::max(ay, std::max(by, cy));
    while (p != a) {
        if (p->x >= x0 && p->x <= x1 && p->y >= y0 && p->y <= y1 &&
            pointInTriangle(ax, ay, bx, by, cx, cy, p->x, p->y) &&
            area(p->prev, p, p->next) >= 0) return false;
        p = p->next;
    }
```
This is a pure speed optimization — the bbox predicate is implied by `pointInTriangle` (with `>= 0` inclusive edges the bbox test can never exclude a point `pointInTriangle` would accept), so **output is bit-identical**. Variant A above is what I validated.

